{"summary":"Accepted 3 amendments and rejected 3. The accepted items identify real correctness or validation issues in zero-DO substrate handling and the geometry-scaling regression tests. The rejected items rely on outdated code snapshots or ignore already-committed geometry-scaling behavior, alkalinity regression tests, and the existing split between TAN-oxidation and nitrate-production tracing.","votes":[{"amendment_id":"fr-1-8c8de1cf","decision":"accept","rationale":"`compute_o2_penetration_depth_cm` currently returns full layer depth when `do_mg_per_cm3 <= 0.0`, which inverts the model at zero surface oxygen. `effective_o2_penetration_depth_cm` also treats `<= 0.0` as \"uncomputed,\" so a legitimate 0 cm penetration cannot be represented at all. That is a real correctness bug in anoxic cases; the fix just needs an explicit uninitialized representation that is safe for persistence."},{"amendment_id":"fr-1-588146f5","decision":"accept","rationale":"The test module and bead acceptance criteria both say the scaling comparison should hold within 20%, but peak TAN and DO range are actually asserted with a 65% tolerance. The inline comment explains why these metrics are sensitive, but it does not justify changing the requirement from 20% to 65%. As written, the test no longer enforces the stated contract, so either the tolerance or the documented requirement needs to change."},{"amendment_id":"fr-1-e742e865","decision":"accept","rationale":"`adult_shrimp_count` already scales with filled volume through auto-stocking, so multiplying per-adult daily feed by `size_scale.powf(2.5).min(2.0)` adds an extra size-dependent load on top of the density scaling. That makes the parity test hard to interpret, because concentration differences can come from deliberately different nitrogen input rather than from geometry-scaled defaults. If that heavier-load scenario is intentional, it needs explicit justification and documentation."},{"amendment_id":"fr-1-e342ebd3","decision":"reject","rationale":"Current code already implements the geometry-aware startup scaling this amendment says is missing. `scale_hardware_to_geometry` scales filter flow and heater watts from geometry, `build_plant_guilds` scales plant biomass by footprint, and `apply_startup_overrides` auto-stocks shrimp from filled volume. The remaining 200 lph and 50 W values in type defaults are just base defaults that scenario materialization overwrites, so this amendment is based on an outdated snapshot."},{"amendment_id":"fr-1-00cf5af0","decision":"reject","rationale":"The tracked runtime guard still only enforces N/C residuals, but that matches the design of the conserved N/C/O element ledger; alkalinity is recorded as stage metrics rather than as a conserved element. More importantly, committed regression coverage already exists in `crates/tank_core/tests/nitrification_alkalinity.rs`, so the claim that the only alkalinity-focused test file is untracked and CI will miss regressions is incorrect. A stronger runtime alkalinity invariant could be a future enhancement, but the defect as stated is not present."},{"amendment_id":"fr-1-1e3d685c","decision":"reject","rationale":"Current code does not expose a single ambiguous `total_n_nitrified_mg` trace. `NitrogenCycleOutput` explicitly separates `tan_oxidized_mg` as the alkalinity-driving TAN-oxidation basis from `nitrate_produced_mg_n` as the amount that actually reached nitrate, and engine tracing logs both. That split already addresses the nitrite-accumulation ambiguity described here."}]}